{"name":"Bridal Veil","hex":"#e7e1de","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bridal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wedding feast or festival; a wedding."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a bride, or to wedding; nuptial."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bridal"},{"term":"veil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something hung up or spread out to hide or protect the face, or hide an object from view; usually of gauze, crepe, or similar diaphanous material.","Anything that partially obscures a clear view."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dress in, or decorate with, a veil.","To conceal as with a veil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/veil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bridal Veil, Oregon","blocks":[{"text":"Bridal Veil is a virtual ghost town located in Multnomah County, Oregon, United States. It was established in the 1880s during a logging boom by a logging company as it harvested timber on nearby Larch Mountain to be a company mill town around a sawmill. It had a close relationship with the logging town of Palmer for the first 50 years of its history. As of November 2011, all that remains of the town is a post office and a cemetery. The site is located near the west end of the Columbia River Gorge."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Train_load_of_larch_as_seen_from_cab_%284587520512%29.jpg/330px-Train_load_of_larch_as_seen_from_cab_%284587520512%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridal_Veil%2C_Oregon"},{"title":"Veil","blocks":[{"text":"A veil is an article of clothing or hanging cloth that is intended to cover some part of the head or face, or an object of some significance. Veiling has a long history in European, Asian, and African societies. The practice has been prominent in different forms in Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. The practice of veiling is especially associated with women and sacred objects, though in some cultures, it is men, rather than women, who are expected to wear a veil. Besides its enduring religious significance, veiling continues to play a role in some modern secular contexts, such as wedding customs."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Chief_Vestal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veil"},{"title":"Bride","blocks":[{"text":"A bride is a woman who is about to be married or who is a newlywed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Wed-dress-001.jpg/330px-Wed-dress-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bride"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg/500px-USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg/1280px-USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg/500px-USPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bridal Veil post office in 2024","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSPS_Bridal_Veil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"